  "slides": {
    "0": {
      "t": "The Divergence Theorem",
      "s": "The electric field leaving a closed surface is set by the charge inside it. The more charge, the stronger the field making a run for it through the surface."
    },
    "1": {
      "t": "Magnetic Lines Never Part",
      "s": "Magnetic field lines always stick together and have neither a start nor an end. That's why there are no magnetic monopoles: a north pole always comes packaged with a south."
    },
    "2": {
      "t": "The Earth's Potential",
      "s": "The gravitational potential at a point is the sum of every nearby mass doing its bit. Each bit of mass lends a hand, and the farther off it sits, the less it matters."
    },
    "3": {
      "t": "A Tall Bridge in France",
      "s": "Stress inside a material is a tensor, so its value depends on the axes you go with. This formula shifts the stress components into another coordinate system using the rotation matrix Q."
    },
    "4": {
      "t": "Summer Season",
      "s": "Temperature changes for two reasons: swept along by the flow (advection) and spread through conduction (diffusion), plus any heat source. This equation rolls the three of them into one."
    }
  },
